# Remove the console keymap patch and the raw-key decoder. Both exist because on Linux the kernel translates scancodes into keycodes and a program wanting different mappings edits that keymap. On Samsara the PS/2 driver already delivers UTF-8 characters to a pty, so there is no keycode layer for a shortcut to live in. setupSysKey() now reports the degradation through the existing keymapFailure path, and setRawMode() deliberately never sets mRawMode: upstream sets it before checking whether the ioctl succeeded, which here would route input into a decoder with no keymap and silently swallow every keystroke.
#
# Generated with `git diff` against the pinned fbterm commit
# 419c6be55413a7069fbcc66883d58caa94ba3933. Regenerate with
# ports/fbterm/build.sh; never edit by hand.

diff --git a/src/input.cpp b/src/input.cpp
index f6ab87b..3336c52 100644
--- a/src/input.cpp
+++ b/src/input.cpp
@@ -97,83 +97,64 @@ void TtyInput::switchVc(bool enter)
 	tcsetattr(STDIN_FILENO, TCSAFLUSH, &tm);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Samsara: the console keymap patch is gone.
+ *
+ * Upstream installs fbterm's keyboard shortcuts (Shift+PageUp, Ctrl+Alt+1, and
+ * the rest) by *editing the kernel's console keymap* through the KDGKBENT and
+ * KDSKBENT ioctls -- reading each entry's old value, writing a new one, and
+ * putting the old values back on exit. That is a system-wide change: it affects
+ * every console on the machine, not just fbterm, and it needs privilege to
+ * undo.
+ *
+ * None of that has any referent here, and the reason is structural rather than
+ * a missing syscall. The console keymap exists because on Linux the *kernel*
+ * translates a scancode into a keycode, and a program that wants a different
+ * mapping has to change the translator. On Samsara the PS/2 driver already
+ * delivers UTF-8 characters to the input server, which forwards them to a pty,
+ * and a pty is a byte stream. There is no keycode layer for a shortcut to live
+ * in, so there is nothing to patch and no way to express one: a shortcut on
+ * Samsara has to be recognised from the character stream, not installed
+ * somewhere upstream of it.
+ *
+ * So the shortcuts are unavailable, and this function reports that rather than
+ * pretending. `keymapFailure` is the same flag upstream sets when the ioctl
+ * fails, and showInfo() below already turns it into the user-facing message
+ * "keyboard shortcuts will NOT work", so the degradation is visible rather than
+ * silent -- which is the property that matters. Declaring struct kbentry and the
+ * two request numbers just to have calls that always fail would have produced
+ * the same result with more code and a false impression that the mechanism is
+ * present.
+ */
 void TtyInput::setupSysKey(bool restore)
 {
